      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nAdds a regression test for #36580, which fixed MRV2 slot mapping under CUDA-graph padding but added no test. No open PR adds one.\n\n## Claims\n\n- After a longer batch, the padded slots of a shorter batch must be `PAD_SLOT_ID`. Before #36580 they kept the longer batch's slots, so padding tokens wrote into KV cache in use.\n- The new test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n\n## Validation\n\n`pytest tests/v1/worker/test_gpu_block_table.py` on an RTX 3050 Ti Laptop GPU:\n\n| Build | Result |\n|---|---|\n| main (4b47db92) | 20 passed |\n| main with #36580 reverted | 1 failed (the new test), 19 passed |\n\nThe revert restores the pre-fix padding start, `positions.shape[0]`. The failure:\n\n```\nassert [20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, ...] == [20, 21, 22, -1, -1, -1, ...]\nAt index 3 diff: 23 != -1\n```\n\nI also ran the same steps as a script against the actual builds on an A40: 156e3355 (before #36580) gives `[20, ..., 27]`, and 9efc3bdc (#36580) gives `[20, 21, 22, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1]`.\n\nCI runs it in the existing `pytest -v -s v1/worker` step in `.buildkite/test_areas/misc.yaml`.\n\n## Details\n\n- No existing test pads past the real tokens: each sets `num_tokens_padded` to the token count.\n- The bug raises no error; it only corrupts KV. On main, the last program of the `num_reqs + 1` grid pads from the real token count, so shrinking the grid or moving that start offset silently brings the bug back.\n- `block_table.py` has changed 22 times since #36580, including three fixes for stale block-table rows (#49757, #56734, #61174).\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\ncc @njhill\n",
